=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The FRR BGP daemon (`bgpd`) and its watchdog (`watchfrr`) are not running on `leaf_router_0_1`. Only `zebra`, `mgmtd`, and `staticd` are present in the process table.

**Mechanism:** Without `bgpd`, leaf_router_0_1 cannot establish or maintain any BGP sessions. It is therefore unable to advertise its directly-attached host subnet `10.0.1.0/24` to its spine peers. Both `spine_router_0_0` and `spine_router_0_1` show this neighbor permanently in the **Active** (trying-to-connect) state. Because the prefix is never advertised, no other router in the fabric installs a route to `10.0.1.0/24`. When `pc_0_0` sends traffic to `pc_0_1` (10.0.1.2), `leaf_router_0_0` finds no route and returns an **ICMP Destination Net Unreachable**, producing 100% packet loss.

**Localization:** `leaf_router_0_1` — specifically the absence of the `bgpd` process and its supervisor `watchfrr`. All five other routers in the fabric are healthy; `spine_router_0_0` is fully established with every peer it can reach and is not at fault.

**Root cause:** The FRR BGP daemon (`bgpd`) crashed or was killed on `leaf_router_0_1`, taking down all BGP sessions from that leaf and withdrawing `10.0.1.0/24` from the fabric.

**Fix:** Restart FRR on `leaf_router_0_1` (e.g., `systemctl restart frr`). This will bring `bgpd` and `watchfrr` back, re-establish BGP sessions with both spine routers, re-advertise `10.0.1.0/24`, and restore end-to-end reachability between `pc_0_0` and `pc_0_1`. Do **not** restart `spine_router_0_0` — it is functioning correctly.
=== END ===